INSTITUTE FOR DEMOCRATIC SYSTEMS FAILURE

WHITE PAPER SERIES No. 71-P — Expanded Edition

THE SENATE HAS ENTERED THE COMMENTS SECTION

On the Conversion of Public Office into Monetised Content, Political Conflict into Platform Revenue, and Representative Government into a Permanent Engagement Strategy

⸻

Executive Summary

The Institute has identified an emerging difficulty in the organisation of democratic life: the people responsible for governing the country are increasingly difficult to distinguish from the people making videos about how the country is finished.

Both announce catastrophe. Both cultivate followers. Both require fresh material. Both appear on the same screen, surrounded by the same advertisements, competing for the same attention.

Occasionally, one of them has access to the Home Office.

This paper examines the consequences of placing public representatives inside commercial communications systems that select, distribute and sometimes financially reward content according to audience behaviour. Its central concern is the institutional arrangement: political authority, audience cultivation, commercial revenue and algorithmic distribution operating within the same environment.

The satirical diagnosis is straightforward:

We have placed Parliament inside a content factory and expressed surprise that it has started manufacturing content.

The serious question is more demanding.

Should platforms such as X and YouTube permit elected representatives, political parties and campaigning organisations to participate on the same commercial terms as entertainment creators? Should their political communications qualify for monetisation, paid visibility or engagement-driven recommendation? Should some social spaces exclude these actors altogether?

Public office creates responsibilities that exceed the maintenance of an audience. Political discourse concerns decisions affecting people’s rights, security and material circumstances.

Its governing standard should therefore be something more substantial than:

“People got absolutely fucking furious, so we showed it to another million.”

