1. The Senate Discovers Premium

Consider an imaginary Roman Senate supplied with smartphones.

A senator rises to discuss the grain supply. His statement is accurate, proportionate and accompanied by an explanation of transport difficulties.

Unfortunately, the audience leaves after eleven seconds.

His communications adviser recommends a different opening:

THEY ARE STARVING ROME.

The senator reposts. This time, people remain.

One citizen demands an explanation. Another accuses the senator of having caused the shortage. A third insists grain shortages are a Carthaginian invention. The senator’s opponent replies with an insult concerning his masculinity.

The adviser reports excellent results.

“But they are arguing,” says the senator.

“Correct.”

“They do not appear to understand the proposal.”

“Understanding is not one of the available metrics.”

“Has any grain arrived?”

“We don’t have a dashboard for that.”

By the afternoon, the senator has acquired thousands of followers and announced a second statement concerning the terrifying developments he will reveal tomorrow.

The grain remains elsewhere.

The Institute calls this administrative substitution: the replacement of progress on a problem with progress in the public performance of having a problem.

The imaginary senator has discovered an important advantage of the new system. An unresolved shortage can sustain several weeks of content. A resolved shortage may require him to find another subject.

His adviser recommends caution before delivering the grain.

