2. Everybody Has a Reply Box. Everybody Does Not Have the Same Power.

A social-media interface offers an attractive visual proposition: everyone appears to be participating on roughly equal terms.

There is a profile picture, a name, a message and a collection of buttons.

The resident complaining about a policy and the minister responsible for it occupy similarly shaped rectangles.

The rectangles conceal an important difference.

One participant can complain about the exercise of state power. The other may help exercise it.

Giving both participants a reply button does not equalise their authority, resources or responsibilities. It merely standardises the furniture.

This matters when political figures adopt the posture of ordinary people “just saying what they think”. An elected representative addressing a mass audience is also making use of a position obtained through public institutions.

The office does not disappear when the official opens an app.

Nor should its standards disappear because the exchange takes place beneath a video of a dog failing to catch a sausage.

The Institute proposes a simple administrative test:

Would this statement require explanation if delivered from a government lectern?

If the answer is yes, posting it beside a laughing emoji should not provide constitutional dry cleaning.

