3. The Constituency Has Been Replaced by the Audience

A constituency and an audience are different kinds of relationship.

A representative owes duties to constituents who dislike them, disagree with them and will never vote for them.

An audience can be cultivated through shared tastes, loyalties and antagonisms. Its members can arrive from anywhere. Its continued attention becomes a measure of success.

The institutional difficulty begins when the second relationship displaces the first.

Consider a hypothetical representative choosing between explaining an unpopular compromise and producing a clip in which that compromise is denounced as betrayal.

The explanation may help constituents understand the decision. The denunciation may help the account grow.

These outcomes can diverge.

A person can become more successful as a political content creator while becoming less useful as a public representative.

Under such conditions, even the meaning of “accountability” can drift. Instead of accounting for decisions to the people affected by them, the representative accounts for their tone to the people most likely to applaud.

The audience asks whether they fought hard enough.

The constituents may have been asking whether the bus service will return.

The Institute recommends that these questions cease being treated as interchangeable.

