4. The Ministry of Engagement

Imagine two communications prepared by the same fictional parliamentary office.

The first explains a funding decision, identifies its limitations and sets out the next stage of scrutiny.

The second reads:

THEY ARE BETRAYING YOU.

The second possesses several production advantages. It requires less explanation, accommodates almost any subject and gives the audience an enemy before troubling it with a mechanism.

There is empirical evidence for the attraction of this kind of political conflict. A 2021 study of approximately 2.73 million posts from US congressional and news-media accounts found that posts concerning the opposing political group were shared or retweeted about twice as often as posts concerning the author’s own group. References to the opposing group were the strongest engagement predictor among those examined. 

The institutional inference is that a politician seeking reach has a reason to notice which kind of communication travels.

An attention reward can become a communication lesson.

The office learns to place the accusation first, the qualification elsewhere and the actual proposal in a document nobody has opened.

The next briefing becomes shorter. The next enemy becomes clearer. The next thumbnail contains a more distressed facial expression.

Eventually, the representative’s communications strategy resembles a fire alarm whose performance is assessed by how many people run around.

The Institute has provisionally renamed this department the Ministry of Engagement.

Its responsibilities include generating concern, measuring concern and expressing concern that concern has not yet reached its full potential.

