5. The Permanent Emergency Subscription

The Institute’s model political broadcaster begins each morning with an emergency.

Monday: the country has been betrayed.

Tuesday: institutions have collapsed.

Wednesday: the truth can finally be revealed.

Thursday: the public must wake up.

Friday: the broadcaster announces that everything is considerably worse than the previous four emergencies suggested.

At the weekend, subscribers receive exclusive analysis.

This fictional arrangement illustrates a conflict between resolving a public problem and maintaining a product built around that problem.

A functioning institution should welcome evidence that a threat has diminished. A channel whose audience arrives for escalating warnings must then decide what tomorrow’s episode is about.

The difficulty does not require an invented crisis. A real problem can still be selected, framed and repeated according to its commercial usefulness.

A serious incident can be discussed as an incident, with evidence and proportion.

It can also be packaged as the latest confirmation that every institution is corrupt, every opponent complicit and every moment decisive.

The second version offers excellent opportunities for a series.

Under the Institute’s proposed classification, this becomes Emergency as a Service.

The customer is never quite safe enough to cancel.

