6. The Double Dividend: Political Advantage and Commercial Value

Monetisation introduces more than one beneficiary.

A political figure may gain visibility or support. An eligible commentator may receive creator income. A platform may earn advertising or subscription revenue. A campaigning operation may convert attention into donations or recruitment.

These are separate routes. A useful analysis must follow each one.

YouTube’s published monetisation options, for example, include advertising revenue, a share of Premium subscription revenue, channel memberships and paid interaction features such as Super Chat and Super Thanks, each subject to eligibility requirements. These mechanisms give audience attention several possible financial destinations. 

Consequently, checking whether an MP personally receives a platform payout addresses only part of the issue.

Consider a politician whose provocative statement generates a chain of monetised reaction videos. Even with no direct payment to the original speaker, the surrounding system can acquire a commercial interest in reproducing the conflict.

One account condemns the statement. Another celebrates it. A third condemns the condemnation.

Everybody produces a thumbnail.

The public argument becomes a source of material for several businesses at once.

In the Institute’s hypothetical balance sheet, the politician gains exposure, the commentators gain content and the platform gains activity.

Whether the public gains an accurate understanding remains a separate accounting question.

Regrettably, that department is understaffed.

