7. The Algorithm Becomes an Unofficial Editor

There are three distinct decisions involved in platform communication.

A statement can be permitted to appear, selected for wider distribution, and made eligible to generate income.

These decisions should not be collapsed into a single argument about whether someone is allowed to speak.

Publishing a parliamentary statement is one thing. Automatically recommending it to people who never sought it out is another. Attaching financial rewards to the attention it generates is a third.

Each involves a different exercise of platform power.

The politician may have obtained their office through an election. The recommendation system did not.

Yet, wherever distribution affects political visibility, the representative acquires a second institution to satisfy: the system deciding what other people will encounter.

Its approval may depend on signals with no necessary relationship to the quality of the political argument.

This creates an extraordinary possibility: a representative can be democratically authorised to speak, but commercially trained in how to be heard.

The Institute describes the resulting arrangement as government by elected officials, edited for distribution by unelected performance criteria.

The algorithm requires no seat in Parliament.

It merely helps determine which parts of Parliament arrive in everyone’s pocket.

