8. The Constitutional Function of Bollocks

Our fictional minister has now been asked to explain a difficult decision.

He replies:

“Grow a pair.”

The Institute’s legislative analysis has so far failed to establish how this resolves the funding question.

Nevertheless, the statement performs several useful functions within an attention contest. It is short, easily repeated and substitutes an assessment of somebody’s personal toughness for an explanation of the issue.

The audience can choose a side without reading anything.

This is political argument redesigned as a dominance performance.

One participant is strong. Another is weak. One “fights”. Another “backs down”. A compromise becomes humiliation. A correction becomes surrender.

Under this vocabulary, evidence risks becoming an unfortunate interruption to the bout.

The relevant standard should concern conduct and reasoning rather than anatomical theatre. A furious, well-supported challenge can perform a public service. A calm, immaculately dressed falsehood can do the opposite.

The problem is not a shortage of good manners.

It is the substitution of performance for responsibility.

A country cannot be competently administered through a continuous audition for the hardest bastard in the comments section.

