9. Where the Satire Becomes a Governance Question

The serious concern is the feedback mechanism.

When a communication attracts attention, receives wider distribution and produces political or financial returns, its author has an incentive to repeat the characteristics associated with that success. Where those characteristics include hostility, alarm or humiliation, the platform can reward behaviour that conflicts with responsible public communication.

Research provides grounds for examining this mechanism rather than treating it as merely an objection to online tone.

A preregistered audit published in PNAS Nexus in 2025 found that Twitter’s engagement-based ranking amplified emotionally charged and politically hostile content relative to a reverse-chronological baseline. The political posts selected by the algorithm also failed to better satisfy users’ stated preferences. What generated engagement and what users said they wanted to see were not equivalent. 

A separate study published in Nature in 2026 reported a seven-week experiment conducted among US X users in 2023. Switching users from a chronological to an algorithmic feed increased engagement and shifted several measured political attitudes towards more conservative positions, without detectable effects on self-reported partisanship or affective polarisation. The distribution mechanism itself had measurable political consequences in that setting. 

These findings make platform design a legitimate subject of democratic scrutiny.

The question is not adequately addressed by asking individual politicians to behave better. The rules governing financial rewards, recommendation and account privileges also require examination.

A system that rewards a behaviour must account for its role in producing that behaviour.

