10. Existing Rules Already Recognise Part of the Problem

The starting point should be the rules platforms actually publish.

X

X’s Original Content Rewards programme, whose rollout begins on 8 September 2026, describes payments based on qualified impressions from Premium users. Its terms exclude elected officials, political parties, people working for government institutions and people engaged in election-related activity from participation. Political and government organisations are also excluded in the programme’s eligibility guidance. 

That establishes an important existing boundary around direct creator payments.

The next questions concern its enforcement and scope: which accounts are covered, how proxy arrangements are detected, and what happens to the commercial and recommendation systems surrounding political content.

A payment restriction and a distribution restriction perform different jobs.

YouTube

YouTube’s published advertiser-friendly guidelines already restrict monetisation for categories including incendiary or demeaning material, harassment and certain harmful misinformation. Its framework also allows contextual distinctions for reporting, education and debate. Those are content-based advertising rules rather than, in the documents examined here, a comparable general exclusion based on holding elected office. 

YouTube also describes recommendations as using several signals—including viewing behaviour, survey responses and feedback—with additional considerations for authoritative information and borderline content. Its system should therefore be assessed against its actual objectives and implementation, rather than treated as identical to X’s. 

A relevant precedent

TikTok expressly makes its designated government, politician and political-party accounts ineligible for creator monetisation programmes. It also imposes political-advertising restrictions and prohibits campaign-fundraising solicitations by those accounts. 

Political status is therefore already treated as a reason for different platform privileges.

The policy question is how comprehensive, consistent and independently verifiable those distinctions should become.

“Suitable for advertising” and “suitable for the exercise of public responsibility” are different standards.

