12. A Proposed Civic-Use Standard

For platforms retaining political actors, this paper proposes the following framework.

Political accounts should operate outside creator monetisation

Covered accounts should receive no engagement-based creator payments, advertising revenue shares, paid-message income or subscription income attached to the exercise or pursuit of public office.

The restriction should follow control and benefit. A party-controlled channel should not escape it by adopting a media-style name. An official should not be able to move the same activity into a nominally separate company and preserve the same incentives.

The public function is the relevant fact.

The platform’s own monetisation should also be addressed

Removing the politician’s payout leaves a further question: should the platform sell advertising against the political communication itself?

A meaningful civic category should be ad-free at the point of consumption and should exclude the use of those interactions for commercial advertising profiles.

Otherwise, the platform may continue to gain from maximising the controversy while presenting the politician’s lack of direct earnings as a complete solution.

This proposal would require transparent definitions of the relevant page, feed placement and viewing session. The commercial boundary must be technically inspectable.

Political communication should be available by deliberate choice

Citizens should be able to search for representatives, subscribe to their updates, examine their records and share their statements.

Distribution should prioritise those deliberate choices rather than inferred susceptibility to provocation.

A civic feed could provide chronological updates from selected accounts, searchable archives and a separately chosen discovery service with published criteria. A user should also be able to keep official political broadcasting out of their ordinary social feed.

Discoverability matters, particularly for candidates without an established audience. A transparent candidate directory or user-selected election feed offers a different mechanism from awarding reach according to the intensity of a reaction.

Paid reach should not purchase civic prominence

Political accounts should not gain preferential civic distribution through premium account tiers, paid boosts or commercial partnerships.

Identity verification should establish who is speaking. It should not purchase a louder microphone.

Political advertising, where a service chooses to permit it, should sit within a separately identified and publicly auditable system rather than blend into ostensibly ordinary political participation.

Official information should remain accessible and accountable

A civic category should preserve public records, identify account control and make corrections visible alongside the original statements.

Essential public-service notices would require their own clearly defined route, particularly for emergencies. Access to a flood warning should not depend on enthusiasm for the government issuing it.

Parliamentary speeches, voting explanations and constituency information should remain accessible without a paid membership or a compulsory commercial account.

The standard should make public communication easier to inspect, rather than simply harder to encounter.

Responsibility should attach to the office

Political accounts should meet published conduct standards addressing targeted harassment, deliberate deception and misuse of institutional authority.

Decisions should include reasons, supporting evidence and an appeal route. Penalties should be predictable and proportionate, with repeated evasion capable of resulting in exclusion.

The rules should apply across parties and levels of popularity. A platform standard that can be suspended for its owner’s preferred politician is an arrangement of patronage, not a standard.

