13. Independent Speech, Proxy Accounts and Enforcement

The boundary should be defined by political role, control, financing and agency.

A journalist, researcher, comedian or ordinary citizen should not acquire political-actor status merely because their work supports or criticises a party. Independent scrutiny requires room to operate, including sustainable ways of funding reporting.

Equally, payment or party control should not disappear behind the label “independent creator”.

This makes disclosure central. Paid political partnerships, beneficial ownership and organised account operation should be visible where they determine which rules apply.

Enforcement must also be open to scrutiny. Relevant reporting would include the number of covered accounts, monetisation exclusions, reinstatements, appeals, detected proxy arrangements and exceptions granted.

Recommendation audits should distinguish audience-requested exposure from platform-added exposure. They should examine whether political accounts obtain additional distribution because of hostility, misleading presentation or unusually intense negative reactions.

The platform’s public description of its intentions is not enough. The relevant question is how its systems behave.

Any use of legislation would require a further, separate assessment of legal authority, expression rights and safeguards. The immediate proposal here is a published platform-governance standard, with equally explicit expectations for the conduct of public representatives.

