14. Success Must Be Measured Against Public Outcomes

A reform cannot be judged solely by whether political engagement falls.

Reduced interaction could reflect less manufactured conflict. It could also reflect reduced access to useful information.

Evaluation should therefore examine whether people can still find their representatives, understand decisions, encounter candidates, scrutinise claims and raise concerns. It should also examine unwanted political exposure, harassment, correction visibility and the distribution of reach among established and emerging participants.

The relevant comparison is between different communication arrangements—not between a busy platform and a quiet one.

A successful civic system would make it easier to obtain information intentionally and harder to convert involuntary emotional reaction into political advantage.

It would also distinguish citizens from revenue opportunities.

A person who repeatedly opens an argument because they are distressed by it has produced a measurable behaviour. That behaviour should not automatically be treated as an instruction to supply more of the same.

Attention is evidence that something captured a person. It is not, by itself, evidence that the person benefited.

