Conclusion: Public Office Requires a Different Standard

The image of a senator arguing in a comments section is funny because it brings two roles into obvious conflict.

One role requires deliberation, explanation and responsibility for consequences.

The other rewards performance before an audience.

When the performance can also generate money, commercial visibility or algorithmic promotion, the conflict becomes an institutional question rather than a matter of taste.

A political communication may be profitable because it helps people understand a decision. It may also be profitable because it frightens them, humiliates an opponent or keeps an unresolved conflict permanently active.

A responsible platform standard must be able to distinguish these possibilities and examine the incentives it creates.

Serving politicians and political organisations should not simply inherit the commercial privileges of entertainment creators. Their participation should be governed by explicit rules covering monetisation, paid reach, recommendation, disclosure and conduct. Social networks should also be able to establish spaces from which institutional political campaigning is excluded.

The public should be able to discuss politics without every discussion becoming an opportunity for political figures to acquire followers. It should be able to hear from representatives without being drawn into an advertising system built around the resulting confrontation.

Above all, democratic communication should not become dependent on whether a private distribution system finds it sufficiently stimulating to promote.

An elected mandate authorises representation.

A view count measures exposure.

A payment records a commercial transaction.

None should be mistaken for either of the others.

Citizens are not an engagement resource. Public office is not a creator category. And democratic discourse should not be governed by the profitability of provoking it.
